---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="astro-seo-enforcer demo — a compliant home page"
  description="The home page of a small Astro site that keeps the astro-seo-enforcer integration enabled during every build so that SEO regressions fail fast."
>
  <h1>astro-seo-enforcer demo site</h1>
  <p>
    Every page in this site is written to satisfy the default rule set: a unique
    title of the right length, a meta description within range, exactly one
    <code>&lt;h1&gt;</code>, a sensible heading outline, semantic landmarks and
    link text that makes sense on its own. The site is deliberately tiny, but it
    exercises the parts of the integration that only show up once several real
    pages exist side by side on disk.
  </p>
  <h2>What the build does</h2>
  <p>
    The integration parses the generated HTML inside the
    <code>astro:build:done</code> hook. It reads the files Astro wrote to the
    output directory, not the source <code>.astro</code> components, so what it
    checks is exactly what a crawler would download. If any rule fails,
    <code>astro build</code> exits with a non-zero status and the continuous
    integration job turns red. Because this demo sets
    <code>failOn: "warning"</code>, even advisory findings stop the build, which
    keeps the example pages honest.
  </p>
  <h2>Why the pages carry real copy</h2>
  <p>
    Several checks only make sense against a page with genuine content. The
    thin-content rule counts the words inside the main region and complains when
    a page reads like a template with a variable swapped in. The near-duplicate
    check compares the main content of every page against every other and flags
    the ones that share almost all of their text. To keep those rules quiet, each
    route here carries a few paragraphs of its own prose rather than a single
    placeholder sentence.
  </p>
  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <p>
    Read the <a href="/about/">project background</a> for the full story of why
    the integration exists, or open the <a href="/blog/first-post/">first
    article</a> to see a nested route pass the same checks from one level deeper
    in the directory tree.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
